========================================================================================================
P1.  HOMOGENEOUS ELASTICITY  ->  VUS AT CHANCE, LEVEL STILL RECOVERED
========================================================================================================
                            world  true_mean  mean_eps  oracle_bias  true_sd     vus  chance  ceiling  vus_norm
homogeneous (all three terms off)    +1.6000   +1.0060      -0.5940  +0.0000 +0.1764 +0.1667  +0.2310   +0.1507
        heterogeneous (sd = 0.55)    +1.7294   +1.2998      -0.4296  +0.6997 +0.1709 +0.1667  +0.2478   +0.0516

  With a homogeneous elasticity the posterior is the SAME for every customer -- the
  baseline demand s(x) cancels out of b_k = pi_k s_k / sum_j pi_j s_j -- so there is
  nothing for a classifier to discriminate on and VUS sits at 0.1764 against a
  chance level of 0.1667.  The elasticity is nonetheless recovered to
  -0.5940.  A model can be at chance VUS and exactly right about the answer.
========================================================================================================
P1b.  DOES `weight_cap` FLATTEN THE DEMAND CURVE?
========================================================================================================
Winsorising the unit weights truncates the largest-quantity rows -- and those are
disproportionately the CHEAP arm, so the cap should bias the demand response flat.

